  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nRidging over the Great Lakes up to James Bay will continue to fold \neast today and tonight with surface high pressure stretching from \nQuebec down along the Atlantic coast into the Carolinas. Meanwhile, \nmultiple potent shortwaves embedded in the main flow will progress \nover parts of the region through the middle of the week. The first \nwave currently over the northern Great Plains will punch across \nOntario/far northern Great Lakes tonight as a second wave works \noverhead by Wednesday afternoon/evening. \n\nForecast Details:\n\nPrimary forecast concern in a relative sense will be the return of \nrain chances later today into Wednesday. Rain shield out ahead of \nthe main wave will continue to work east over Lake Michigan this \nafternoon and is expected to keep losing steam with time. Said rain \nis already on the periphery of favorable low/mid-level moisture and \nis likely to outpace this environment -- eventually falling apart \nover northern Michigan this afternoon and evening, resulting in \nperhaps light rain for some areas and even no rain at all for others \nas falling precip struggles to reach the ground through the \naforementioned dry air. Best chances for rain will come late tonight \nand especially Wednesday morning across northern lower Michigan as \nbetter moisture and support aloft works into the area. As discussed \nby the previous forecaster, meager buoyancy aloft on the order of a \nfew hundred J/kg of MUCAPE with moist adiabatic profiles will \nlargely limit thunderstorm chances. That said, a few embedded storms \ncannot be completely ruled out. Rain chances are expected to \ndiminish Wednesday afternoon.\n\nAside from rain, southwest winds look to increase ahead of an \napproaching cold front through Wednesday morning. Sustained winds \nbetween 10-20 mph with gusts to 25-35 mph are expected from late \nmorning into early evening Wednesday, with the potential for locally \nhigher gusts near the Lake Superior and Lake Michigan shorelines as \nwinds shift to west/northwest behind the front later Wednesday. \nWinds look to taper off some Wednesday evening. \n\nLooking ahead, dry weather is expected through the second half of \nthe week as high pressure builds back in. Cooler temperatures are in \nstore for Thursday as highs struggle to get out of the 60s for some \nduring the afternoon, but look to rebound heading into the weekend. \nOvernight lows may also dip well into the 40s late Thursday \nnight/early Friday morning. Rain chances may return later this \nupcoming weekend as troughing works across the region, but the \nassociated surface cyclone looks to be far displaced from Michigan \nand could result in most of the area staying dry through the first \nhalf of next week.",
